;;; Syntax probe — arg-kinds.
;;;
;;; Every argument kind a fact can carry: a name, digits (a name, so `7` and
;;; `07` are two), `1..5` and `1..*` (names: the range token went at M1f
;;; S1f.5.3, and S1f.5.4's name rule reads each as the name it spells), a name
;;; with connectors and decorators, a nested fact, and a stored negation as a
;;; value. The string, the bare `_` and the variable it carried until M1f
;;; S1f.5.5's swap are refused at the parse (S1f.5.2 and S1f.5.1), Q-M1f.2
;;; (a)'s rewrite.
;;;
;;; Its `(solve …)` holds `r` out of the hypotheses (the user's ruling of
;;; 2026-11-08 on S1f.5.5's review stop, item 1, *"Scope the solve"*): the
;;; fixture's point is the node kinds as written, and unscoped its universe's
;;; commitments pass the state route's 2^128 limit, which stays parked
;;; (S1f.5.4's § Notes). R is {∅}.
;;;
;;; One of the eighteen node-kind fixtures. As corpus entries they are
;;; rendered under every parse view and every op, and each rendering is
;;; digested by `corpus_shapes.md5`, so a change to how any of these
;;; shapes is drawn moves a digest. They exist because no *puzzle*
;;; contains these shapes.

(relation r T T :why "{?1} r {?2}")
(r 7 07)
(r 1..5 1..*)
(r $x a-b_c.d)
(r (nested a b) c)
(r (not (nested a b)) c)

(solve :no-hypothesis (r))
